docs(manual): start the central end-user manual - install, recovery, safety

Phase 2 begins. The central NebulaOS repository now has a wiki/ with 23 pages:
6 real ones and 17 honest stubs.

Written:

- Home.md, routing by task rather than by component.
- _Sidebar.md, navigation across all ten mission sections.
- Safety-First.md, led by the hazard that can permanently damage a printer: a
  stock Creality power-on can write old firmware back into the mainboard.
- Installing-NebulaOS.md, both routes, in the mission's procedure format.
- Recovery.md, all four ways back, least drastic first, with the one option that
  erases everything clearly marked.
- Going-Back.md, returning to stock or OpenKE.

Verified rather than copied. The existing wiki said the automatic trial fallback
waits "about 5 minutes" and gives up after "three times". Both are correct -
TRIAL_SECONDS = 300 and TRIAL_ATTEMPTS = 3 in nebulaos_osconfig/image.py at
firmware 82e1320 - but that was established by reading the source. Two accurate
documents agreeing with each other is not evidence. Also confirmed: both firmware
docs the recovery page links to exist, swap_slot is a real API method, and
usb-set-boot-slot.sh is where the page says it is.

The untested stock-touchscreen install route gets its own "How much of this is
tested" section near the top of the install page, with a table of which route is
tested and which is not. A reader deciding whether to flash their printer needs
that before the steps, not as a footnote after them.

Two new tools:

- check_links.py gates broken links, missing images and pages unreachable from
  the sidebar. Its docstring states what it cannot prove - that a page says
  anything true, or that a screenshot shows the screen it claims to - because a
  green check otherwise invites the reader to assume more than it earned.
- set_status.py changes inventory statuses one id at a time, so a status change
  is deliberate and visible rather than a hand edit to JSONL where a typo is not.

On the stubs (D-007): a full sidebar immediately produced 17 links to pages that
do not exist. Broken links were not an option in a safety-critical manual, and
trimming the sidebar would have hidden the manual's shape and made the gaps
invisible. So each unwritten page exists and says so, and the 11 whose topics are
already covered well in the NebulaScreen wiki link straight there. A stub is not
documentation and is not counted as such: those items stay missing, and the
dashboard reports 4 documented of 544, which is the truth.

Recorded as the next real risk: 59 items require a screenshot and none exist.
Whether the SDL simulator can supply them decides if that is solvable without a
printer, and it should be answered soon rather than at the end.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
